<mapreducescript scriptid="customscript_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
  <description>Trader Screen cache builder for CWP ARCH (subsidiary 9). Builds On Hand from lot balances; the other five buckets are structurally zero until ARCH sales orders and purchase orders exist in the account.</description>
  <isinactive>F</isinactive>
  <name>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</name>
  <notifyadmins>F</notifyadmins>
  <notifyemails></notifyemails>
  <notifyowner>T</notifyowner>
  <scriptfile>[/SuiteScripts/mcgi_services/trader_screen/entry_points/mr/mcgi_mr_trader_screen_cache_arch.js]</scriptfile>
  <!-- No script custom fields on purpose. The MTL fork carries four, but this
       builder reads none of them: the subsidiary is a constant, there is no
       delta mode to threshold or force past, and no lot costing yet to point at
       an accounting book. Declaring parameters the code never reads is how a
       deployment ends up looking configurable when it is not. Add them when the
       code actually consumes them. -->
  <scriptdeployments>
    <scriptdeployment scriptid="customdeploy_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
      <buffersize>1</buffersize>
      <concurrencylimit>1</concurrencylimit>
      <isdeployed>T</isdeployed>
      <loglevel>DEBUG</loglevel>
      <queueallstagesatonce>T</queueallstagesatonce>
      <runasrole>ADMINISTRATOR</runasrole>
      <!-- SCHEDULED hourly, deliberately, 2026-08-18.
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           This looks like it contradicts the standing rule "never enable the
           trader-cache MR's scheduled deployment". It does not. That rule is
           about the IND builder, and it exists for three specific mechanisms,
           ALL THREE OF WHICH ARE ABSENT HERE — verified against both files on
           2026-08-18:

             1. IND throttles on TS_LAST_RUN_TIMESTAMP with a non-atomic
                read-then-write, so two runners both decide "not throttled" and
                both start a FULL rebuild. This builder has NO throttle state at
                all (IND: 13 references to LAST_RUN, ARCH: 0).
             2. IND's summarize READS TS_SUMMARY, merges, and writes back, so
                interleaved runs mean last-writer-wins on a partial merge and
                one run's delta silently vanishes. This summarize never reads
                SUMMARY back — it writes a complete payload built from the
                output iterator, so a concurrent run can only write the same
                bytes.
             3. IND's summarize self-reschedules onto
                runtime.getCurrentScript().deploymentId, so enabling a second
                deployment forks an immortal chain. This builder contains no
                N/task, no task.submit and no deploymentId reference (IND: 1 and
                3 respectively).

           So ARCH is a stateless, idempotent FULL rebuild on a SINGLE
           deployment with concurrencylimit 1. It was scheduled because the
           opposite failure was real and observed: TTL is 4h and nothing
           refreshed the cache, so the summary endpoint returned CACHE_MISS
           overnight and the screen had no data until someone pressed a button.

           TWO RULES IF YOU CHANGE THIS:
             - Never add a second deployment to this script. One chain, one
               deployment. That is what keeps mechanism 3 inapplicable.
             - Keep TTL_SUMMARY/TTL_DETAIL above the refresh interval with
               margin. Hourly against a 4h TTL is 4x, so three consecutive
               failed runs are survivable. Lowering the TTL to "match" the
               interval is the exact mistake that caused the IND blackout.

           `repeat` is an ISO 8601 duration — PT1H. Not a word enum. Taken from
           what SDF actually wrote after the change was made in the UI; do not
           guess this value. `starttime` is UTC: 11:00Z is 07:00 PT. -->
      <status>SCHEDULED</status>
      <title>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</title>
      <yieldaftermins>60</yieldaftermins>
      <recurrence>
        <daily>
          <everyxdays>1</everyxdays>
          <repeat>PT1H</repeat>
          <startdate>2026-08-18</startdate>
          <starttime>11:00:00Z</starttime>
        </daily>
      </recurrence>
    </scriptdeployment>
  </scriptdeployments>
</mapreducescript>
